// # The Coral Module and Import Specification

// This document defines the complete, finalized specification for Coral's module, path-resolution, and import system.

// ---

// ## 1. Physical Layout & Architecture

// Coral separates package implementation source files from their public interface definitions using a **manifest layer** (`.crl` files).

// ### Global Library Shelf (`~/.coral/`)

// The compiler maintains a global, compiler-owned directory structure for installed libraries and the standard library:

// ```text
// ~/.coral/
//   lib/
//     std.crl          <- export manifest (one per installed package)
//     serde.crl
//   std/
//     math/math.crl    <- actual implementation files
//     io/io.crl
//   serde/
//     json/json.crl
//     yaml/yaml.crl

// ```

// * **`~/.coral/lib/`**: A flat directory of manifests. Each manifest filename corresponds directly to a package name (e.g., `std.crl` represents package `std`).
// * **Implementation Source**: Lives outside `lib/`, addressed via relative path strings declared *inside* the manifest.

// ### Package Distribution

// When a package is downloaded, it ships with an author-written manifest at its root:

// ```text
// serde/
//   lib.crl            <- author-written manifest
//   json/json.crl
//   yaml/yaml.crl

// ```

// Upon installation, the compiler:

// 1. Copies `serde/lib.crl` to `~/.coral/lib/serde.crl`.
// 2. Copies package sources to `~/.coral/serde/`.
// 3. Resolves internal manifest path strings relative to this new install location.

// ---

// ## 2. The Three Import Mechanics

// Coral provides three distinct ways to bring code into scope, avoiding any collision between local files and global libraries.

// ### A. Local Explicit Path Import

// Imports directly via a relative path string pointing to a source file.

// ```c
// import "src/math/core.crl" { square };
// square(5);

// ```

// ### B. Project Root Import (`file::`)

// Imports via project-configurable roots defined in `coral.json`.

// ```c
// import file::math::core { square };
// square(5);

// ```

// ### C. Global Manifest Import (`import(lib)`)

// Bypasses local roots entirely. It looks up a flat manifest name directly in the compiler-owned shelf (`~/.coral/lib/<name>.crl`), completely eliminating collision risks between local directories and global packages.

// ```c
// import(lib) std::math { pow };
// pow(2, 10);

// ```

// ---

// ## 3. Module Aliasing vs. Symbol Renaming

// * **`as`** is strictly reserved for renaming **individual symbols** inside symbol lists `{ ... }`.
// * **`mod <alias> = import ...`** is used for binding entire modules to a local or public namespace.

// ```c
// // Private module alias
// mod m = import(lib) std::math;
// m::pow(2, 10);

// // Public re-export for consumers of this module
// pub mod m = import(lib) std::math;

// ```

// ---

// ## 4. Complete Syntax and Grammar Reference

// ```c
// // --- Imports ---
// import "<path string>" [{ <symbol>[, <symbol> as <alias>]* }];
// import <root>::<segment>::... [{ <symbol>[, <symbol> as <alias>]* }];
// import(lib) <manifest>::<segment>::... [{ <symbol>[, <symbol> as <alias>]* }];

// // --- Module Aliasing & Re-exports ---
// mod <alias> = import "<path>";
// mod <alias> = import(lib) <manifest>::<path>;

// pub mod <alias> = import "<path>";
// pub mod <alias> = import(lib) <manifest>::<path>;

// // --- Visibility and Definitions ---
// pub <type> <name>(...) { ... }
// pub struct <name> { ... }
// pub const <type> <name> = ...;

// ```

// ---

// ## 5. Comprehensive Usage Examples

// ### 1. Local project import, explicit path

// ```c
// import "src/math/core.crl" { square };
// square(5);

// ```

// ### 2. Local project import with root shorthand

// ```c
// import file::math::core { square };
// square(5);

// ```

// ### 3. Compiler-provided stdlib, drilled to one symbol

// ```c
// import(lib) std::math { pow };
// pow(2, 10);

// ```

// ### 4. Stdlib, module-qualified

// ```c
// import(lib) std;
// std::pow(2, 10);
// std::read_line();

// ```

// ### 5. User-downloaded library

// ```c
// import(lib) serde::json { parse, stringify };
// data = parse(raw);
// out = stringify(data);

// ```

// ### 6. Conflict resolution using symbol `as` aliasing

// ```c
// import(lib) std::math { sqrt };
// import(lib) geometry { sqrt as geo_sqrt };

// sqrt(9);        // std::sqrt
// geo_sqrt(16);   // geometry::sqrt

// ```

// ### 7. Conflict resolution between local and library symbols

// ```c
// import file::physics { velocity };
// import(lib) std::motion { velocity as std_velocity };

// velocity(t);
// std_velocity(t);

// ```

// ### 8. Re-exporting a library symbol through a local module

// ```c
// // src/mathx/mod.crl
// pub mod pow = import(lib) std::pow;

// // elsewhere
// import file::mathx { pow };
// pow(2, 10);

// ```

// ### 9. Wildcard import (flattening public symbols of a module level)

// ```c
// import(lib) std::math { * };
// pow(2, 10);
// sqrt(4);

// ```

// ### 10. Bare import, fully qualified use

// ```c
// import(lib) std::math;
// import file::physics;

// std::pow(2, 10);
// physics::velocity(t);

// ```

// ### 11. Multi-hop re-export chain

// ```c
// import(lib) std::pow;
// pow(2, 10);
// // resolution path: lib/std.crl -> mod math -> math.crl export table -> pow

// ```

// ### 12. Aliasing an entire re-exported module

// ```c
// mod m = import(lib) std::math;
// m::pow(2, 10);

// ```